    section: "A", questionRef: "2022 HL Section A Q6(a) and (b)",
    questionText: "Firms in Perfect Competition are said to be Price-Takers. On the diagrams below label and illustrate: (i) What happens to the market equilibrium as more firms enter the market? (ii) What happens to the individual firm’s price as more firms enter the market? Explain each of your diagrams below. Firms can survive in the short run even if they are making a loss. Explain this statement.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal","notation":"15","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 15,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-diagrams", kind: "point", verbatim: "The Market The Firm", marks: null, contextNote: "Compare both labelled diagrams with the official solution: market supply shifts right from S1 to S2, and the firm’s horizontal D = AR = MR line falls from P1 to P2. The scheme prints 4, 2 and 2 beside (a) and no separate value beside (b) or (c), so the complete route is assessed against its 15 marks.\n\nBoth editions require the diagrams and their explanations plus this short-run answer. Irish mark cells are displaced; assess the complete 15-mark route without assigning separate marks to its diagrams or alternative.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-market", kind: "point", verbatim: "Explanation for the market: As firms outside the industry become aware of the SNPs earned in the industry these firms will enter the industry which they can do as there are no barriers to entry. As these firms enter the market supply will shift to the right from S1 to S2. This quantity supplied increases from Q1 to Q2 and decreases the price from P1 to P2.", marks: null, contextNote: "Explain the market diagram: supernormal profit attracts entry, supply shifts right, quantity rises and price falls.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-firm", kind: "point", verbatim: "Explanation for the firm: As the individual firm is described as a price-taker they must accept the price established by the industry, thus their price also decreases from P1 to P2.", marks: null, contextNote: "Explain the firm diagram: a price-taker accepts the lower market price.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-alternative", kind: "point", verbatim: "• In the short run if AR is greater than AVC • The firm is making a loss but once it covers its average variable costs it will contributes towards its fixed cost then the loss from shutting down would be greater than the loss from continuing to operate.", marks: null, contextNote: "Answer (b) as well as both diagrams and their explanations.", openList: true },
    ],
