    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2022 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2022-hl-q16-c-i", topicId: "economics-1-3", conceptId: "minimum-pricing-on-fast-fashion",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2022 HL Q16(c)(i)",
    stem: "The fashion industry produces 10% of all humanity’s carbon emissions. It is the second largest consumer of the world’s water supply and pollutes the oceans. 85% of all textiles go to landfill each year. Research shows that people are buying more clothes now than ever but are only keeping them for half as long.",
    questionText: "Explain, with the use of a fully labelled diagram, how the introduction of minimum pricing (above the market equilibrium price) on fast fashion will impact on the market for fast fashion.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"10 + 5","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 15,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-diagram", kind: "point", verbatim: "Fully labelled diagram", marks: 10, contextNote: "The diagram, 10 marks: place Pmin above the equilibrium price PE, mark QD and QS at the minimum price with the surplus between them, the original equilibrium E and QE, both curves and both axes. The official diagram is shown in the figure.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-effects", kind: "point", verbatim: "Effect on price Through imposing a minimum price on these products, the price will be legally forced upwards from PE to Pmin. Effect on demand With the increase in price there will be a fall in demand for these products from QE to QD. Effect on market This will result in a surplus of fast fashion clothes as indicated above.", marks: 5, contextNote: "The effects, 5 marks: price rises from PE to Pmin, quantity demanded falls from QE to QD and quantity supplied rises to QS, leaving a surplus. The scheme’s “fall in demand” is a fall in quantity demanded, a movement along the curve.", openList: true },
    ],
