    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2023 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2023-hl-sa-q5-b", topicId: "economics-0-1", conceptId: "efficient-inefficient-and-impossible-points",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2023 HL Section A Q5(b)",
    stem: "The diagram below shows the Production Possibilities Frontier for a digital manufacturing company that produces both laptops and tablet devices.",
    questionText: "Justify each of your choices you made in (a) above.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"6","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "Efficient refers to a production point where the firm is making the best possible use of scarce resources. i.e. it is producing as much as it can given their current resources. This can be seen anywhere on the boundary – Point C.\n\nInefficient refers to a production point where the firm is wasteful of scarce resources. i.e. it is possible to produce more tablets and laptops given their current resources. This can be seen anywhere inside the boundary – Point A\n\nImpossible refers to a production point which the firm cannot currently produce given their current scarce resources. In order to produce this level of output they need to increase their factors of production. This can be seen anywhere outside the boundary – Point B.", marks: 6, contextNote: "Justify all three, 6 marks overall: on the frontier, inside it, outside it.\n\nIrish source correction: C is efficient because it lies on the frontier; A is inefficient because it lies inside; B is currently impossible because it lies outside. The Irish scheme inconsistently substitutes P for C and misnames the inefficient point.", openList: true },
    ],
