    id: "econ-2023-hl-sa-q7-a", topicId: "economics-1-0", conceptId: "landlords-leaving-and-the-rental-market",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2023 HL Section A Q7(a)",
    stem: "Landlords are leaving the Irish housing market in their thousands, blaming excessive taxation, rent control and constantly changing laws.",
    questionText: "Show on the diagram below how this development will alter the rental market equilibrium position. Explain your answer.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"3 + 3","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-diagram", kind: "point", verbatim: "S1", marks: 3, contextNote: "Diagram, 3 marks: shift supply LEFT from S to S1; the equilibrium price rises from Pe to Pe1 and the quantity falls from Qe to Qe1. The official diagram is shown in the figure.\n\nIrish graph labels: É denotes demand in the paper. In the solution drawing, supply shifts left from S to S1 and rent rises from Pe to Pe1. Follow the drawing; the scheme prose incorrectly refers to S1→S2 and Pe1→Pe2.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-explanation", kind: "point", verbatim: "• As landlords leave the market it reduces the supply of properties in the market. • The supply curve will shift to the left from S1 to S2. • The equilibrium price will increase from Pe1 to Pe2.", marks: 3, contextNote: "Explain, 3 marks: fewer landlords means fewer rental properties supplied, so the equilibrium rent rises. The scheme’s bullets name the curves S1 to S2 and Pe1 to Pe2; its diagram labels them S to S1 and Pe to Pe1.", openList: true },
    ],
