    id: "econ-2023-hl-q12-a-iii", topicId: "economics-2-0", conceptId: "long-run-equilibrium-in-perfect-competition",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2023 HL Q12(a)(iii)",
    questionText: "Explain, with the use of a fully labelled diagram (including the axes), the long run equilibrium of a firm in perfect competition.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"9 + 8","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 17,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-diagram", kind: "point", verbatim: "Price Quantity P C Q MC AC D/AR/MR E", marks: 9, contextNote: "Diagram, 9 marks at 1 per label: Price, Quantity, P, C, Q, MC, AC, D = AR = MR and E. MC cuts the horizontal D = AR = MR line at the minimum of AC (E), so P = C. The official diagram is shown in the figure.\n\nIrish diagram note: the horizontal-axis label C denotes quantity/output, called Q in the explanation. Keep output distinct from C used for costs on the vertical axis. English Q notation is also shown in the solution.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "• Equilibrium is at point E (where MC = MR). • Firms produce quantity Q. • They sell the output at price P. • Costs occur at level C. • The firm earns normal profit as AR = AC. • The firm is efficient as costs are at a minimum point of the ATC curve.", marks: 8, contextNote: "Explanation, 8 marks with no separately priced steps: E where MC = MR, output Q, price P, cost C, normal profit because AR = AC, and production at minimum average cost.", openList: true },
    ],
