    id: "econ-2023-hl-q16-a-ii", topicId: "economics-1-0", conceptId: "free-school-transport-and-the-car-market",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2023 HL Q16(a)(ii)",
    stem: "The Irish Government has provided free school transport for the 2022/2023 school year.",
    questionText: "Demonstrate, with the use of a fully labelled diagram the impact this new scheme will possibly have on the market equilibrium for petrol/diesel cars.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"7 + 8","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 15,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-diagram", kind: "point", verbatim: "The demand curve will shift to the left from D1 to D2.", marks: 7, contextNote: "Diagram, 7 marks: demand shifts LEFT from D1 to D2; the equilibrium price falls from P1 to P2 and the quantity from Q1 to Q2. The official diagram is shown in the figure.\n\nIrish scheme correction: D1→D2 is a leftward shift of demand (éileamh), not supply (soláthar). Free school transport substitutes for car travel, reducing equilibrium price and quantity.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-explanation", kind: "point", verbatim: "• The demand curve will shift to the left from D1 to D2. • Why? Buses are substitute goods for cars. Some households will feel reduced need for a car as school transport has been provided. They will switch to the free transport. • The equilibrium price will decrease from P1 to P2. • The equilibrium quantity will decrease from Q1 to Q2. (iii) The rapid introduction of this scheme created an additional demand of 6,000 bus tickets in late September 2022 after schools had opened.", marks: 8, contextNote: "Explanation, 8 marks: free school buses are a substitute for cars, so demand for cars falls and the equilibrium price and quantity fall.", openList: true },
    ],
