    id: "econ-2024-hl-sa-q2-b", topicId: "economics-3-2", conceptId: "why-full-employment-is-an-aim",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2024 HL Section A Q2(b)",
    stem: "According to Central Statistics Office (CSO) data, Ireland’s seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for October 2023 was 4.8%. This may be regarded as full employment.",
    questionText: "Explain, giving two reasons, why full employment is an economic aim of the government.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"2 @ 3","answer":2,"ofParts":4,"perPart":3}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A reason full employment is an aim — any two", marks: 6, contextNote: "Two reasons, 3 marks each. Answer either (b) or (c).", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":3,"options":["Increased direct/indirect tax revenue – When individuals are in employment, they contribute to the exchequer’s tax take through direct tax (PAYE and USC). When individuals are employment also, they have more disposable income and consequently can spend more. This generates indirect tax revenue (VAT) for the exchequer.","Decreased social welfare payments – If individuals are in employment that means that the government does not have to pay them social welfare which reduces the government’s current expenditure.","Boosts demand / economic growth in the economy – If individuals are in employment, they should have more disposable income to spend in the economy. This boosts aggregate demand in the economy.","Reduction in poverty: if people are in employment they are earning an income and this increases their standard of living reducing poverty levels within the state."]} },
    ],
