    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2025 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2025-hl-sa-q9-b", topicId: "economics-2-0", conceptId: "the-kinked-demand-curve",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2025 HL Section A Q9(b)",
    stem: "The diagram below represents the long run equilibrium of a firm operating in an oligopoly market.",
    questionText: "With reference to the diagram above, explain the shape of the curve labelled 2.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal","notation":"6","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-before", kind: "point", verbatim: "Before the kink – if a firm increases its price, competing firms will not follow by raising their prices. Customers will then switch to competitors, who are keeping their prices unchanged. As a result, quantity demanded will decrease by a greater percentage than the price increase / the firm will lose a lot of consumers. Hence this part of the demand curve is relatively price elastic.", marks: null, contextNote: "Above the kink: rivals do not follow a price rise, so demand is relatively price elastic.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-after", kind: "point", verbatim: "After the kink – if a firm reduces its price, competing firms will match the price cut to avoid losing their customers. Since all firms are lowering prices together, the firm will not gain many new customers. This means quantity demanded will increase by a smaller percentage than the price reduction. Hence, this part of the demand curve is relatively price inelastic.", marks: null, contextNote: "Below the kink: rivals match a price cut, so demand is relatively price inelastic. The scheme gives one 6 for the explanation, with no published split.", openList: true },
    ],
