    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2025 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2025-hl-q16-c-ii", topicId: "economics-1-5", conceptId: "staying-open-at-a-short-run-loss",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2025 HL Q16(c)(ii)",
    stem: "The graphic below shows the number of companies in Ireland's hospitality sector that became insolvent in the first half of 2023 and of 2024. Note: Insolvency occurs when a company cannot meet its financial commitments and may cease to operate.",
    questionText: "In the short run a company may remain in business even if it is making a loss. Explain the meaning of this statement.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"5","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 5,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "Even if making a loss, a firm should continue to operate in the short run if its total revenue (TR) exceeds its total variable costs (TVC) / if its average revenue (AR) is greater than its average variable costs (AVC). This is because any revenue above variable costs helps offset fixed costs. The firm's losses from continuing to operate will be less than the losses it would incur by shutting down entirely.", marks: 5, contextNote: "Explain why a loss-making firm stays open in the short run, 5 marks.", openList: true },
    ],
