    year: 2026, level: "higher",
    paperFileid: "LC034ALP000EV",
    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2026 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2026-hl-sa-q5-a-irrational", topicId: "economics-1-1", conceptId: "bnpl-and-rational-behaviour",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2026 HL Section A Q5(a) — irrational consumer behaviour",
    stem: "Some financial companies, such as Klarna, offer buy now, pay later (BNPL) agreements for some online purchases in Ireland. These agreements allow consumers to either pay upfront or to spread the cost over time, sometimes with excessive added interest.",
    questionText: "Do you think BNPL companies, such as Klarna, encourage rational consumer behaviour or irrational consumer behaviour? Indicate your answer below by ticking (✓) the relevant box and justify your answer. Justify the choice: IRRATIONAL CONSUMER BEHAVIOUR.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"1 @ 6","answer":1,"ofParts":5,"perPart":6}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "A reason BNPL encourages irrational choices", marks: 6, contextNote: "One developed justification for the irrational consumer behaviour position, 6 marks. The scheme credits both positions; these are its examples, not a closed list.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":6,"options":["Small instalments make purchases appear cheaper – BNPL agreements can make purchases appear cheaper by highlighting small monthly payments rather than the total price. This may cause consumers to focus on the instalment amount and underestimate the overall cost of the purchase. As a result, they may buy items they would not have purchased if they had to pay the full amount upfront.","Increased impulse purchases / Present bias – the delayed payment aspect of BNPL allows some consumers to make unplanned / impulsive / emotional purchases now without careful consideration rather than purchasing based on need or affordability. This reflects present bias, where immediate benefits are prioritised over future costs. Cognitive biases like this lead to irrational consumer behaviour.","Overborrowing / Increased debt – if a consumer uses multiple BNPL agreements at the one time, they may take on more debt than they can afford. This represents irrational decision-making and may lead to missed payments or financial difficulties.","Poor understanding of terms and conditions / Low levels of financial literacy – some consumers may not fully understand repayment conditions, penalties, or fees associated with BNPL agreements. Limited financial knowledge may lead consumers to enter borrowing arrangements without fully understanding the financial commitments involved, which lead to irrational financial decisions.","Social media influence / Peer pressure / Herd behaviour– BNPL is widely promoted on social media and used by influencers and possibly one’s peers. This may encourage consumers, especially younger ones, to copy others’ behaviour without carefully considering affordability or need. This may lead to irrational purchasing decisions."]} },
    ],
